`,s.jsx(J,{items:[{q:"Define a(i) and b(i), then read off what each of the three sign cases means.",a:"a(i) is the mean distance from the point to the other members of its OWN cluster; b(i) is the smallest mean distance to any other cluster. a < b gives s positive — correctly placed. a = b gives s = 0 — on the boundary. a > b gives s negative — the point is on average nearer a competing cluster and should probably move."},{q:"A point has a = 3.2, b = 1.8. Compute s and say what to do.",a:"s = (1.8 - 3.2)/max(3.2, 1.8) = -1.4/3.2 = -0.44. It is strongly misplaced: it sits closer on average to a different cluster than to its own. A cluster full of such points usually means K is wrong or the clusters are not spherical."},{q:"Why can silhouette handle non-spherical clusters better than the elbow?",a:"Because it is computed from pairwise distances between points, not from distances to a centroid. A crescent-shaped cluster has a centroid that may not even lie inside it, which wrecks WCSS-based reasoning, while point-to-point distances still capture that its members are mutually close."},{q:"Mean silhouette is 0.62 but the plot shows one cluster entirely below the average line. What does that tell you?",a:"The mean is being propped up by the well-separated clusters while one cluster is genuinely poor — probably two real groups merged, or a group that overlaps a neighbour. The aggregate number hides it; this is exactly why you inspect the plot rather than trusting the single score."},{q:"Give the practical workflow for choosing K.",a:"Run K from 2 to 10 with n_init at least 10. Note the elbow in WCSS, the K maximising mean silhouette, the K minimising Davies-Bouldin, and the K maximising Calinski-Harabasz. Choose where two or three agree, then inspect silhouette plots for the top candidates before committing."}]}),`
